#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "bc694d40-d150-55e6-981f-0ca6b5d91e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-10418" :question/problem "A travel agency offers three itineraries, itinerary $1$, itinerary $2$, and $3$. For each itinerary, customers can choose to travel using their own car or the agency's bus.\n\n- Itinerary $1$ is chosen by $25\\%$ of the customers, and $60\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n- Itinerary $2$ is chosen by $30\\%$ of the customers, and $30\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n- Itinerary $3$ is chosen by $45\\%$ of the customers, and $20\\%$ of those customers travel by bus.\n\nIf $65\\%$ of all customers travel with children, and $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a customer travels with children, $B$ be the event that a customer takes the bus, and $I_1, I_2, I_3$ be the event that a customer chooses itinerary $1$, $2$, or $3$, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(I_{1}) = 0.25 & P(B \\mid I_{1}) = 0.6 \\\\ P(I_{2}) = 0.3 & P(B \\mid I_{2}) = 0.3 \\\\ P(I_{3}) = 0.45 & P(B \\mid I_{3}) = 0.2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe disjoint union $I_1 \\sqcup I_2 \\sqcup I_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n$P(I_1)+ P(I_2) + P(I_3) = 0.25+0.3+0.45 = 1$.\n\n- Since $65\\%$ of customers have children, we have\n$P(C) = 0.65$.\n- Since $40\\%$ of customers with children choose the bus, we have\n$P(B \\mid C) = 0.4$.\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer that chooses the bus travels with children, which is represented as $P(C \\mid B)$.\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(C\\begin{vmatrix}B) &= (P(B \\mid C)P(C))/(P(B)) \\\\ = (P(B \\mid C)P(C))/(P(B \\mid I_{1})P(I_{1}) + P(B \\mid I_{2})P(I_{2}) + P(B\\end{vmatrix}I_{3})P(I_{3})) \\\\\n&= ((0.4)(0.65))/((0.6)(0.25) + (0.3)(0.3) + (0.2)(0.45)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0.26}{0.33} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.788\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166697 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "699d9c0c-bb98-5710-8d98-10b6f35ab202" :question/math-academy-id "e-8911" :question/problem "The disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ of events $A_1,A_2,A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_1 \\mid B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$P(B\\begin{vmatrix}A_{1}) = 0.23, \\\\ P(B \\mid A_{2}) = 0.26,\\end{vmatrix}P(A_{2}) = 0.3; P(B \\mid A_{3}) = 0.18, \\mid P(A_{3}) = 0.4$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k \\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(B \\mid A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B)} = \\dfrac{P(B \\mid A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B \\mid A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=3$, and $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup A_3$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP(A_1) + P(A_2) + P(A_3) = 1\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A_{1}) &= 1 - (P(A_{2}) + P(A_{3})) \\\\\n&= 1 - (0.3 + 0.4) \\\\\n&= 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}B) &= (P(B \\mid A_{1})P(A_{1}))/(∑_(i = 1)^(3)P(B \\mid A_{i})P(A_{i})) \\\\ = (P(B \\mid A_{1})P(A_{1}))/(P(B \\mid A_{1})P(A_{1}) + P(B \\mid A_{2})P(A_{2}) + P(B\\end{vmatrix}A_{3})P(A_{3})) \\\\\n&= ((0.23)(0.3))/((0.23)(0.3) + (0.26)(0.3) + (0.18)(0.4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0.069}{0.219} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.315\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "284f5029-19eb-53d4-a3b7-0f66d33b9cc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8912" :question/problem "Potatoes produced by a particular farm come in three different varieties, variety $1$, variety $2$, and variety $3$.\n\n- $36\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $1$, and $20\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$.\n- $17\\%$ of the potatoes are of variety $2$, and $6\\%$ of those potatoes weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$.\n- $47\\%$ of the potatoes are of the variety $3$.\n\nIf $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, $40\\%$ of potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$, and $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, what is the probability that a randomly selected potato from variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a potato is sold to the restaurant, $W$ be the event that a potato weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$, and $V_1, V_2, V_3$ be the event that a potato is of variety $1$, $2$, or $3$, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n$P(V_{1}) = 0.36\\begin{vmatrix}P(W \\mid V_{1}) = 0.2 \\\\ P(V_{2}) = 0.17\\end{vmatrix}P(W \\mid V_{2}) = 0.06; P(V_{3}) = 0.47$\nThe disjoint union $V_1 \\sqcup V_2 \\sqcup V_3$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n$P(V_1)+ P(V_2) + P(V_3) = 0.36+0.17+0.47 = 1$.\n- Since $16\\%$ of potatoes from the entire farm are sold to a local restaurant, we have\n$P(R) = 0.16$.\n- Since $40\\%$ potatoes sold to the restaurant weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$, we have\n$P(W \\mid R) = 0.4$.\n- Since $15\\%$ of potatoes that weigh more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$ are sold to the restaurant, we have\n$P(R \\mid W) = 0.15$.\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen potato of variety $3$ weighs more than $100\\,\\mathrm g$, which is represented as $P(W \\mid V_3)$.\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(R\\begin{vmatrix}W) &= (P(W \\mid R)P(R))/(P(W)) \\\\ P(R \\mid W)|= (P(W \\mid R)P(R))/(P(W \\mid V_{1})P(V_{1}) + P(W \\mid V_{2})P(V_{2}) + P(W \\mid V_{3})P(V_{3})) \\\\ 0.15|= ((0.4)(0.16))/((0.2)(0.36) + (0.06)(0.17) + P(W \\mid V_{3})(0.47)) \\\\ 0.15|= (0.064)/(0.0822 + 0.47P(W\\end{vmatrix}V_{3}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(W \\mid V_3)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.15(0.0822 + 0.47P(W\\begin{vmatrix}V_{3})) &= 0.064 \\\\ 0.0822 + 0.47P(W \\mid V_{3})|= \\frac{0.064}{0.15} \\\\ 0.0822 + 0.47P(W \\mid V_{3})|= \\frac{32}{75} \\\\ 0.47P(W \\mid V_{3})|= \\frac{32}{75} - 0.0822 \\\\ P(W \\mid V_{3})|= \\frac{1}{0.47}(\\frac{32}{75} - 0.0822) \\\\ P(W \\mid V_{3})\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 0.733\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8dac953-8c37-59e7-8fe0-02995240c8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.367" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163769} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a3e076-7caa-5547-9a00-710bdb26a958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.275" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163770} {:answer/id #uuid "0065bdc3-5a85-580a-b646-c0c3f8b3a082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.427" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163771} {:answer/id #uuid "378ab112-fb34-5f2f-a941-6b455f5fc293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.435" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163772} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa843b3-4375-5967-9938-b21632b67e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163773}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e72dc4b4-bf09-5d67-8671-75165506e5e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163768}] :question/id #uuid "b7f933e2-5017-50df-b8ef-7fd429d6b6ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-147126" :question/problem "The disjoint union $A_{1}⊔A_{2}⊔A_{3}$ of events $A_{1}, A_{2}, A_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_{2} \\mid B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(B \\mid A_{1}) = 0.4, & P(A_{1}) = 0.2 \\\\ P(B \\mid A_{2}) = 0.5, & P(A_{2}) = 0.3 \\\\ P(B \\mid A_{3}) = 0.3, & P(A_{3}) = 0.5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
